 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the civil service law, in relation to credit for unused sick\nleave for employees of community colleges",
    "basePrintNo": "S7933",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7933-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §167, Civ Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Civil Service Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7933\n\nSPONSOR: BENJAMIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the civil service law, in relation to credit for unused\nsick leave for employees of community colleges\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nProvides for full restoration of accrued sick leave for a state employee\nwho are separated from state service and regain employment with a commu-\nnity college\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the Civil Service Law to allow for an individual who\nworks for the state and is separated from state service to maintain\ntheir accrued sick leave for purposes of offsetting the cost of vested\nretiree health care if, within one year of the date of separation from\nstate service, the individual regains employment with a community\ncollege.\n\nSection 2 provides for the effect date.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nnone\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrent Civil Service Law and relevant regulations allow a state employ-\nee to bank up to 1400 hours of sick leave, and upon retirement the sick\nleave bank can be used to offset the monthly cost of healthcare provided\nto employees who vest lifetime healthcare coverage. Under the regu-\nlations promulgated by the Department of Civil Service, an employee may\nuse their sick leave bank for this purpose if they retire directly from\nstate service, or they are separated from state service for non-disci-\nplinary reasons and regain state service within one year.\n\nHowever, the definition of state service is limited to state agencies,\nindependently elected bodies, and a select universe of state academic\ninstitutions. This current list does not include community colleges, who\nreceive a substantial amount of operating aid from the state and are\notherwise public employers. The lack of inclusion of community colleges\nis even more peculiar due to the current inclusion of the public academ-\nic institutions run by private entities at Geneva, Cornell, and Alfred.\n\nThis legislation would specifically allow an employee who is separated\nfrom state service to maintain their sick leave bank if they regain\npublic employment within one year with a community college. As a result,\na public employee would not lose their accrued sick leave in the limited\ncase of separation from state service and a return to public employment\nwith a community college.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal. Affected state employees have accrued sick leave pursuant to\ntheir health benefit plan, and the accrual of those benefits should be\nincluded as part of the annual OTPS appropriation for an individual\nagency. The loss of this accrual is essentially a future unplanned\nsurplus for the state. As a result, there should be little new costs to\nthe state for allowing a limited number of public employees affected by\nthis scenario to maintain their sick leave bank to be applied to their\nretiree health care cost.\n\n \nLOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
